            <ArticleTitle>An unusual cause for a painful Birmingham hip resurfacing.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e10-1</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1308/003588413X13511609955454</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The painful total hip arthroplasty requires careful evaluation and investigation. This is usually focused on the prosthesis and adjacent anatomical structures. We present a case report of a 64-year-old man who had a Birmingham hip resurfacing procedure for primary osteoarthritis. His hip pain worsened following the procedure and was under systematic investigation for this. Subsequent investigation for vascular disease revealed a total infrarenal aortic occlusion. An aortobifemoral bypass improved the hip pain and function dramatically, and the patient now has an excellent quality of life.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
